## BioMotion-OSS Core Technology Detailed Design Specification [Official Final Edition]## Chapter 1: Video Analysis Pipeline (AI & Data Extraction Layer)
Defines the algorithm to extract 2D pixel data from general media footage and translate it into 3D joint angle data for robotics applications.
## 1-1. Two-Stage Time-Series Hybrid Learning Mathematical Model

* Macro-Learning (Self-Supervised Learning):
Extracts frame-to-frame differences (optical flow) from vast amounts of raw, unlabelled animal video footage (SNS, web videos, etc.). Utilizing a Video-Transformer architecture, the system pre-trains the network to recognize dynamic contours and bulk locomotion vectors of the animal's entire body. This embeds the macroscopic mass-transfer tendencies into the network before handling individual joint blurs.
* Micro-Learning (Annotation & Fine-Tuning):
Applies human-annotated ground-truth coordinates of specific micro-structures (e.g., 5 reference points along whiskers, 6 joints along the tail) via supervised fine-tuning to the output layer of the pre-trained macro model. Since the macro-learning phase has already established the boundaries of the physical object, the system minimizes edge misidentification even with sparse training data.

## 1-2. Trajectory Prediction & Error Detection Filter Algorithms
To determine whether an AI-predicted coordinate point is an anomaly or tracking error, the system continuously runs a two-stage mathematical filter pipeline:

   1. Stochastic Filtering (Kalman Filter & Likelihood Evaluation):
   Monitors the tracking confidence score ($C_t$) provided by the AI for each joint coordinate output $(X_t, Y_t)$. If $C_t < 0.60$ persists for 3 consecutive frames or more, the system flags the drop as tracking loss (an anomaly). During this range, the coordinates are automatically interpolated using predicted values ($\hat{X}_t$) derived from the immediately preceding velocity vector $\vec{v}_{t-1}$ and acceleration vector $\vec{a}_{t-1}$.
   2. Kinematic Constraint Check:
   Enforces fixed kinematic boundaries hardcoded from animal anatomy (skeletal structure), specifically focusing on maximum allowable inter-joint distances ($L_{max}$) and maximum angular boundaries ($\theta_{max}$).
   $$\sqrt{(X_{parent} - X_{child})^2 + (Y_{parent} - Y_{child})^2} > L_{max}$$ 
   If an output coordinate violates the equation above (implying an impossible bone elongation) or shows unnatural joint hyperextension, the system immediately rejects the frame as a bug, plots it in red on the timeline, and notifies the human operator.

