## Chapter 2: Embedded Control & Hardware Architecture (Chassis Layer)## 2-1. Compliance Control (Actuator Buffer) Implementation Specifications
Real-time stiffness and elasticity configuration across 16 servo actuators (3 axes per limb × 4 limbs, 2 axes for the tail, 2 axes for the ears).

* Torque-Limiting Control:
Synchronizes and transmits a maximum allowable torque parameter (current limit value: $I_{limit}$) alongside target angle positions from the central processing unit to each actuator.
* Standard Locomotion: Sets $I_{limit}$ to a median value, allowing ankle joints to passively deflect (buffer) against surface irregularities.
   * Landing and Leaping: Synchronizes with the exact moment of landing impact (the peak G-force extracted from animal motion data) to instantaneously drop $I_{limit}$. Mechanical shock is absorbed through deliberate joint "relaxation" rather than stressing internal actuator gears.

## 2-2. High-Speed Signal & Communication Protocols

* CAN (Controller Area Network) Bus Integration:
To transmit target angles and torque limits bi-directionally to 16 actuators at ultra-high cycles ranging from 500 Hz to 1 kHz, standard I2C buses are discarded. The architecture mandates a CAN bus (or RS-485 multi-drop configuration). This prevents bandwidth throttling, eliminating tracking delays and limb jitter.

## 2-3. Microcontroller Interrupt Priorities (Control Stabilization)

* RTOS (Real-Time Operating System) Task Prioritization:
Defines strict priority levels within the main microcontroller unit (ARM Cortex-M7 class) for task scheduling and interrupt handling:
* Priority [High]: 16-axis actuator compliance control for fall prevention, along with emergency fire/anomaly sensor detection and immediate interrupt processing.
   * Priority [Low]: Chemical/odor sensor data analysis, chaotic mathematical tracking for ear gestures, and speaker audio output routines.

